WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.292
- Good morning, believe nation.

00:00:01.292 --> 00:00:04.729
Today's message is execute your dream.

00:00:04.729 --> 00:00:06.312
Over to you, Eddie Murphy.

00:00:06.312 --> 00:00:08.270
(rooster crows)

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.937
(hip hop music)

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:19.664
- I knew I wanted to be in show business,

00:00:19.664 --> 00:00:23.248
and I just happened to luck
out and things happened.

00:00:23.248 --> 00:00:28.094
I think you know, you know
what you're supposed to do.

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:29.511
Deep down inside, I think everybody does,

00:00:29.511 --> 00:00:32.542
and a lot of people just
don't go after it, you know?

00:00:32.542 --> 00:00:33.962
Like, most people start out, they say,

00:00:33.962 --> 00:00:36.195
I want to be a this, but
I'm going to get that

00:00:36.195 --> 00:00:38.279
to make sure I have
something to fall back on.

00:00:38.279 --> 00:00:40.021
And what you're doing is
you're setting yourself up

00:00:40.021 --> 00:00:41.623
for failure 'cause you're going,

00:00:41.623 --> 00:00:43.731
there's a possibility that
I'm going to fall back.

00:00:43.731 --> 00:00:45.984
And when you put that out
there, then you fall back,

00:00:45.984 --> 00:00:47.447
but if you just say, hey,
this is what I want to do

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:49.803
and you go do it, you
usually get your stuff

00:00:49.803 --> 00:00:51.893
the way you want it, man.

00:00:51.893 --> 00:00:53.509
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:00:53.509 --> 00:00:54.580
The question of the day today is

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:59.108
what is preventing you from
executing your true big dream?

00:00:59.108 --> 00:01:00.501
What's holding you back?

00:01:00.501 --> 00:01:01.732
Leave it in the comments below,

00:01:01.732 --> 00:01:03.115
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:01:03.115 --> 00:01:05.654
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Lee Geng Yu.

00:01:05.654 --> 00:01:07.667
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:08.700
Your One Word.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.778
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:01:10.778 --> 00:01:12.473
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:01:12.473 --> 00:01:13.530
I believe in you.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.689
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:18.742
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:21.409
And I'll see you again tomorrow
morning for another shot

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:22.242
of Entspresso.

00:01:22.242 --> 00:01:24.909
(hip hop music)

00:01:28.575 --> 00:01:29.595
What's up, believe nation?

00:01:29.595 --> 00:01:31.185
If you want a little more motivation

00:01:31.185 --> 00:01:35.074
on executing your dream, stay
tuned for some bonus clips.

00:01:35.074 --> 00:01:36.997
- My assistant's got a
great thing now in this.

00:01:36.997 --> 00:01:39.147
Once in every four months,
somebody sends an email

00:01:39.147 --> 00:01:40.389
saying, "I've got a huge startup

00:01:40.389 --> 00:01:42.305
"that I want you to invest in,

00:01:42.305 --> 00:01:45.451
"but you've got to sign this NDA," right?

00:01:45.451 --> 00:01:47.657
Which literally every time
gets an email back that says,

00:01:47.657 --> 00:01:49.329
"Fuck you," right?

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:53.299
And the reason is, I'm a
humongous believer that ideas

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:56.341
are shit and that
execution's the game, right?

00:01:56.341 --> 00:01:57.641
We've all got ideas.

00:01:57.641 --> 00:01:59.236
Everybody's got ideas.

00:01:59.236 --> 00:02:01.420
Do you know how many (bleep)
ideas we all have here?

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.361
We can probably sit here
for the next two hours,

00:02:03.361 --> 00:02:06.076
draw them all out, record
them, and predict the next 78

00:02:06.076 --> 00:02:09.025
great startups over the next nine years.

00:02:09.025 --> 00:02:10.574
And?

00:02:10.574 --> 00:02:14.482
So I think the thing that is another theme

00:02:14.482 --> 00:02:18.406
in entrepreneurship is
there is way too much fodder

00:02:18.406 --> 00:02:20.322
brought to the idea.

00:02:20.322 --> 00:02:23.674
Uber was Magic Cab three years earlier.

00:02:23.674 --> 00:02:27.636
Uber is not an idea, Uber
existed, it was called Magic Cab.

00:02:27.636 --> 00:02:31.305
But the guys that executed
it sucked, so they lost.

00:02:31.305 --> 00:02:35.136
So I think, you know, if there's
any level of romance left

00:02:35.136 --> 00:02:38.201
in this room about your idea,
I'd like to suffocate it

00:02:38.201 --> 00:02:40.500
because I think the actual situation

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.434
is what you actually do with it.

00:02:42.434 --> 00:02:43.828
- The way of the entrepreneur

00:02:43.828 --> 00:02:47.995
is actually about looking at
the world and finding problems.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.776
It's actually, some people
think that's a negative mindset,

00:02:54.776 --> 00:02:58.642
but actually I find it is
like, a curious mindset.

00:02:58.642 --> 00:03:00.859
'Cause you're out there
looking for problems,

00:03:00.859 --> 00:03:02.612
looking for things that aren't the way

00:03:02.612 --> 00:03:04.505
they should be or could be.

00:03:04.505 --> 00:03:05.503
Okay, you got a good idea.

00:03:05.503 --> 00:03:09.253
Look, everybody's got
a good idea, you know?

00:03:11.643 --> 00:03:14.037
Okay, everybody has an idea,
not everybody has a good idea,

00:03:14.037 --> 00:03:15.477
but lots of people have good ideas.

00:03:15.477 --> 00:03:17.311
Look, Uber's a good idea.

00:03:17.311 --> 00:03:19.505
You push a button and get a ride.

00:03:19.505 --> 00:03:20.745
I don't know if we were the first person

00:03:20.745 --> 00:03:23.062
to ever think of that idea,

00:03:23.062 --> 00:03:26.229
but we were the first person to do it.

00:03:27.244 --> 00:03:31.037
And so you've got to just go do it then.

00:03:31.037 --> 00:03:33.393
You got to just get
out there and go do it.

00:03:33.393 --> 00:03:36.246
So it's easy to have the
idea, or straightforward,

00:03:36.246 --> 00:03:39.385
but then you've got to have enough guts

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:41.774
to go out there and try.

00:03:41.774 --> 00:03:44.607
And that's the way of the entrepreneur.

00:03:44.607 --> 00:03:45.641
- I think you famously said,

00:03:45.641 --> 00:03:49.562
"Strategy is 5% of the
answer and execution is 95%."

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:53.729
So what does that mean, and
what did you do differently?

00:03:55.738 --> 00:04:00.597
- Yeah, 5% of the challenge is
strategy, 95% the execution,

00:04:00.597 --> 00:04:05.347
this I said it as CEO as Nissan
and I said it also as CEO

00:04:05.347 --> 00:04:08.827
of Renault, and I said it
as Deputy CEO of Renault.

00:04:08.827 --> 00:04:11.497
When I said it the first
time, it was maybe in France,

00:04:11.497 --> 00:04:16.350
and that was to the top
management of Renault in France

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.044
because there was a
tendency in the company

00:04:19.044 --> 00:04:20.751
to spend too much time on strategy

00:04:20.751 --> 00:04:24.210
and leave the execution for other people.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.603
And this is where I said,
"Yeah, strategy is fine,

00:04:26.603 --> 00:04:29.437
it's very important, but it's only 5%."

00:04:29.437 --> 00:04:32.223
If you don't go to execution after this,

00:04:32.223 --> 00:04:34.282
the delivery and the
performance of the company

00:04:34.282 --> 00:04:35.438
is going to be very weak.

00:04:35.438 --> 00:04:37.110
I really, really believe it.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.165
Now, it doesn't mean
strategy's not important,

00:04:39.165 --> 00:04:41.046
but when you establish north,

00:04:41.046 --> 00:04:45.240
and you know exactly where
north is for the company,

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.805
you've done something very important

00:04:46.805 --> 00:04:48.093
because you're giving the direction.

00:04:48.093 --> 00:04:50.194
But if you don't start moving north,

00:04:50.194 --> 00:04:53.944
if you don't start mapping
and establishing milestones,

00:04:53.944 --> 00:04:56.197
you're never going to be getting there.

00:04:56.197 --> 00:04:57.427
You're never going to be getting there.

00:04:57.427 --> 00:04:58.554
- Execute.

00:04:58.554 --> 00:05:02.402
And you need to execute
as fast as possible.

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:03.899
Because the thing at the end of the day is

00:05:03.899 --> 00:05:07.069
whether you feel you
have competitors or not,

00:05:07.069 --> 00:05:08.928
sooner or later you're
going to have someone

00:05:08.928 --> 00:05:10.693
and if you can't move fast enough,

00:05:10.693 --> 00:05:12.794
someone's going to beat you to the punch.

00:05:12.794 --> 00:05:15.128
So make sure whatever you're
doing, you get something out

00:05:15.128 --> 00:05:16.452
within a month or two.

00:05:16.452 --> 00:05:17.508
If it takes you longer than that,

00:05:17.508 --> 00:05:20.490
you're taking way too long.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.407
I created this company called Serph

00:05:23.407 --> 00:05:27.006
and it was in Rockwell,
Texas, right by Dallas.

00:05:27.006 --> 00:05:28.922
And I had this brilliant idea.

00:05:28.922 --> 00:05:31.058
Have any of you guys
heard of the Media Temple

00:05:31.058 --> 00:05:33.462
Grid server solution?

00:05:33.462 --> 00:05:36.898
That was my idea a year
or two before it came out.

00:05:36.898 --> 00:05:39.406
And I spent a million bucks on it

00:05:39.406 --> 00:05:42.099
trying to actually do that
idea before Media Temple.

00:05:42.099 --> 00:05:44.003
The funny thing is I didn't
have a million bucks,

00:05:44.003 --> 00:05:46.325
I actually had to borrow a million bucks,

00:05:46.325 --> 00:05:50.260
and I spent it on business
partners who moved really slow

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:52.665
and really weren't the
best at what they did.

00:05:52.665 --> 00:05:55.521
Within 12 months we launched nothing.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:56.914
They also had some of the problems

00:05:56.914 --> 00:05:58.520
in my previous slide, right?

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.800
Like financial instability,
and they had a family

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.692
and kids of I think like six.

00:06:03.692 --> 00:06:05.527
So I even bought 'em a
home in Rockwell, Texas

00:06:05.527 --> 00:06:08.081
that cost 200 and something thousand.

00:06:08.081 --> 00:06:09.671
Why did I buy it instead of renting it?

00:06:09.671 --> 00:06:13.512
I have no clue, I was stupid and young.

00:06:13.512 --> 00:06:17.514
But nonetheless, I lost
over a million bucks on that

00:06:17.514 --> 00:06:18.347
of borrowed money.

00:06:18.347 --> 00:06:20.086
Not investor money, but borrowed money

00:06:20.086 --> 00:06:22.768
that I had to pay back.

00:06:22.768 --> 00:06:26.356
And it failed miserably because
we never launched anything.

00:06:26.356 --> 00:06:27.656
If you can't launch something,

00:06:27.656 --> 00:06:29.746
somebody like Media Temple's
going to beat you to the punch,

00:06:29.746 --> 00:06:32.323
get a ton of customers,
and make a lot of money.

00:06:32.323 --> 00:06:34.250
So lessons I learned from that?

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:35.748
Set deadlines.

00:06:35.748 --> 00:06:38.151
No matter what, you have to set deadlines.

00:06:38.151 --> 00:06:41.181
People have to be held accountable
to them as well, right?

00:06:41.181 --> 00:06:44.504
You can't just set a deadline,
it passes, nothing happens.

00:06:44.504 --> 00:06:46.046
Someone better be meeting 'em.

00:06:46.046 --> 00:06:48.647
If not, you know, something has to change.

00:06:48.647 --> 00:06:50.771
You also need to make sure they're small.

00:06:50.771 --> 00:06:54.777
If the deadlines, like
milestones, goals, are too big

00:06:54.777 --> 00:06:57.226
for those deadlines, they're
not going to get met.

00:06:57.226 --> 00:06:59.885
So make 'em really small and tangible,

00:06:59.885 --> 00:07:03.206
that way you can see it
really well in advance

00:07:03.206 --> 00:07:05.829
if they're actually
going to be met or not.

00:07:05.829 --> 00:07:08.732
And last but not least,
don't worry about perfection.

00:07:08.732 --> 00:07:11.007
A lot of people are like,
"dude, I'm going to create

00:07:11.007 --> 00:07:14.882
the most perfect version of,"
you know, whatever it may be.

00:07:14.882 --> 00:07:16.250
"That has a lot of problems,

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:17.298
mine's going to be perfect."

00:07:17.298 --> 00:07:19.608
Perfection is overrated,
nothing's ever perfect.

00:07:19.608 --> 00:07:22.046
You're going to continue to have
to iterate, so forth, so on.

00:07:22.046 --> 00:07:24.647
It's never going to be perfect,
there's always going to be issues,

00:07:24.647 --> 00:07:27.250
just get it out there and
start trying to make money.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:29.557
- Well the last and the
most valuable lesson

00:07:29.557 --> 00:07:31.391
as an entrepreneur I learned,

00:07:31.391 --> 00:07:34.769
was about the value of execution.

00:07:34.769 --> 00:07:39.020
Way too many people, way too
many people spend too much time

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:41.004
thinking about the vision.

00:07:41.004 --> 00:07:44.649
We are taught that you have
to build a better mousetrap.

00:07:44.649 --> 00:07:46.774
Every venture capitalist will tell you

00:07:46.774 --> 00:07:48.991
that to be a successful entrepreneur,

00:07:48.991 --> 00:07:51.008
you need to break through innovation.

00:07:51.008 --> 00:07:55.544
Well you have to assume, none
of us, or at least most of us,

00:07:55.544 --> 00:07:58.040
are not the smartest people in the world.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:01.918
There are lots and lots of
great, great inventors out there.

00:08:01.918 --> 00:08:04.460
How are we going to be the person

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.000
who's going to always
build a better mousetrap?

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.389
Well, to be successful, you don't have to

00:08:09.389 --> 00:08:10.584
build a better mousetrap.

00:08:10.584 --> 00:08:13.812
You just have to build
a different mousetrap.

00:08:13.812 --> 00:08:16.900
And as long as you are executing well,

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:19.884
the business always comes
down to block and tackle.

00:08:19.884 --> 00:08:21.602
It's not about the great vision,

00:08:21.602 --> 00:08:24.794
it's always about the great execution.

00:08:24.794 --> 00:08:26.360
And you can always succeed

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.263
by out-executing your competitors,

00:08:29.263 --> 00:08:32.572
not necessarily having the best
product in the marketplace.

00:08:32.572 --> 00:08:34.812
And I think that Microsoft
is a great example.

00:08:34.812 --> 00:08:37.425
Where they took a same product

00:08:37.425 --> 00:08:39.073
that other people may have had,

00:08:39.073 --> 00:08:41.035
but they executed it substantially better

00:08:41.035 --> 00:08:43.466
and today are the market
leader in the industry.

